# Target C: generic isolates and static unit-anchor localization

Date: 2026-10-05. Base main: `ad8809fd96c2c0af9a6de4d965e9ab33efe092c6`.
State: `2026-10-05.audit.59`.
Classification: **proved candidate synthesis awaiting independent audit**;
artifact lifecycle **unreviewed**. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Exact implication and purpose

```
static equal-contact-budget singleton exchange
-> requires a genuinely new, surrounding retained unit-anchor triple
-> canonical isolates can be chosen to avoid all such unit-circle triples
-> static anchors must be positive-contact core vertices
-> closure requires a core unit-circumcircle/clearance theorem,
   or a motion that changes the anchors, or a genuinely collective packet.
```

This is an exclusion of proposed static bridges and a source normalization,
not a packet-existence theorem. No perturbation of the core is used and
no rigidity claim is applied to isolate coordinates.

Dependencies are canonical C=C_* and H=H_* in Section 56, the endpoint
scope in Section 57, and contact-first/diameter-pair-second normalization.
The surrounding-anchor requirement is the independently proved candidate
in `target_c_contact_budget_diameter_exchange_2026-10-05.md`, based on
canonical audit.59's deleted-contact covering.

## 2. Arbitrarily small generic isolate normalization

Let A=V union Z be a canonical C-II source, with V its nonisolated
positive-contact core and Z its contact isolates. All actual diameter
endpoints lie in V: H=H_* and every positively diameter-loaded vertex
has positive contact load. Hence every pair involving an isolate has
strict lower and upper slack.

Keeping V fixed, there is a product neighborhood of the Z coordinates
in which every such pair remains strictly between 1 and D. The original
core contact and diameter pairs stay fixed. Every configuration in this
neighborhood has cardinality n, diameter D(n), exactly the same contact
graph and exactly the same diameter graph. In particular it remains
triangle-free, contact-minimal and diameter-pair-minimal.

For three distinct old labels a,b,c define the polynomial

    F(a,b,c)=|a-b|^2 |b-c|^2 |c-a|^2
             -4 det(b-a,c-a)^2.

A noncollinear triple lies on a unit circle exactly when F=0. Indeed its
squared circumradius is the product of squared side lengths divided by
four times the squared determinant. Collinear distinct triples have
positive product and zero determinant, so do not give an exception.

For every triple containing an isolate, F is a nonzero polynomial in
the free isolate coordinates. Freeze the other two positions at distinct
values and send the chosen free isolate variable to infinity. The first
term has a positive degree-four leading coefficient |a-b|^2 while the
squared determinant has degree at most two. This proves nonidentity;
it does not require the large displacement to be feasible.

Similarly, for every old pair containing an isolate,

    G(a,b)=|a-b|^2-4

is a nonzero polynomial in the free coordinates. There are only finitely
many F and G conditions. A finite union of the zero sets of nonzero real
polynomials has empty interior, so their complement intersects every
open feasible isolate neighborhood. Thus one can choose arbitrarily
small isolate perturbations for which:

1. no triple containing an isolate has unit circumradius;
2. no pair containing an isolate has distance two;
3. all original contacts, diameters, source cardinality and normalization
   remain unchanged.

If Z is empty the statement is vacuous. On a counterexample sequence the
perturbations can be chosen smaller than any prescribed positive
source-dependent tolerances, for example D^(-100). No uniform lower
slack or specific rational coordinate certificate is claimed. Core
geometry and its physical contact faces remain fixed; no exterior
isolate is silently discarded or forced into the force hull.

## 3. Static anchor consequence

Consider a source with the preceding generic isolate choice. Delete one
contact-active vertex and attempt an interior feasible singleton
replacement while fixing every retained point. Audit.59 and the finite
contact-opening argument imply its retained unit neighbors strictly
surround the new point. In particular at least three of them are
noncollinear and lie on its unit circle.

Every such neighbor is in V. If any were an isolate, select that neighbor
and two others on the same circle: the resulting noncollinear old-source
triple would violate the generic F condition. Thus a static singleton
site cannot use old isolates as exact anchors. In particular the forced
near-unit isolated blockers of a primitive all-loaded shell do not
supply a static exact-anchor triple merely because they are close to
unit distance from the fan leaf.

For a proposed triangle-free three-anchor site, each anchor pair must
be strictly farther than one apart; a unit anchor edge together with the
inserted center would create a triangle. Strict surrounding means the
three circular angular gaps are all less than pi; the absence of unit
anchor edges makes them strictly greater than pi/3. These are genuine
extra geometric requirements, beyond finding any three core points.
The site must also clear every other retained source point and lie
strictly inside the full source hull.

The generic pair-distance condition additionally eliminates opposite
unit anchor pairs involving an isolate. It is not used to rule out
collective packets: their inserted points may be anchors, and old
isolates may support one or two contacts in a larger moving framework.
Nor does this result constrain a finite motion that deliberately creates
unit-circle equalities among changed source coordinates.

## 4. Old degree-three neighbors cannot be the new anchors

Let p have three distinct old unit-contact neighbors a,b,c. They are
noncollinear, since a line intersects the unit circle centered at p in
at most two points. Their unique circumcenter is p. Therefore any point
x satisfying

    |x-a|=|x-b|=|x-c|=1

must equal p. If p is loaded, p is an actual source-hull extreme vertex,
so this center is not an interior replacement and preserves its old
diameters. A three-anchor exchange cannot preserve those same three
contacts and obtain a different center.

In the exact degree-three fan geometry, retaining just its two fan
neighbors would allow the reflected center p'. But the third old unit
neighbor z has |z-b|<D for the fan carrier b whenever it is strictly
inside the carrier fan triangle. The exact reflection identity gives

    |p'-z|^2-1=-(D^2-|z-b|^2)/D^2<0.

Thus the third contact becomes an overlap. This conditional identity
explains why audit.58's degree-two scope cannot be extended by simply
retaining the extra contact. No unpromoted extraction of a large
population of degree-three fans is assumed.

## 5. Weak capacity inside a preserved short wall

The canonical short-wall proof has an exact weak-clearance corollary.
Let C be any simple cyclic polygon with n<=6 sides of length at most
one. If x is strictly inside it and |x-v_i|>=1 for all wall vertices,
then n=6 and C is a regular unit hexagon centered at x.

**Proof.** Set r_i=|x-v_i|>=1. For the principal oriented increment theta_i
along each side, the cosine law and

    r_i^2+r_(i+1)^2-r_i*r_(i+1)-1
    =(r_i-r_(i+1))^2+r_i*r_(i+1)-1>=0

give |theta_i|<=pi/3. The edge cannot pass through x: its length would
then be at least r_i+r_(i+1)>=2, contrary to its length at most one.
The simple interior winding is +/-2pi. Thus

    2pi <= sum |theta_i| <= n*pi/3 <= 2pi.

Every inequality is equality. There are six edges, all angle increments
have the same sign and absolute value pi/3, and the displayed nonnegative
expression vanishes for each adjacent pair. Therefore all radii are one
and all edges are one. This is the asserted regular unit hexagon. QED.

Inserting x in that equality case creates a unit equilateral triangle
with each adjacent wall pair. Hence no triangle-free replacement packet
can put even one site inside an intact retained unit wall of at most six
edges, even if exact wall contacts are permitted. A pentagon has no weak
site at all. A hexagon's only possible weak site forces a new triangle.
This concerns triangles in the replacement, not a triangle in the original
source; it cannot by itself prove Level I. A strict sub-D competitor is
allowed to contain triangles and could still use a weak hexagon site if
all its other constraints are certified.

For static triangle-free contact-budget exchanges, an intact physical
pentagon or hexagon therefore cannot furnish the new anchored site.
A candidate must lie in a larger retained wall, an outer region with its
own clearance proof, or a face whose boundary guards were explicitly
deleted. If the deletion breaks the short wall, the winding argument
no longer applies. In particular it is not applied to an old face whose
boundary contains the deleted loaded vertex.

## 6. Loaded anchors cannot surround a unit center at large diameter

Use canonical Section 58's full-source midpoint bound: every actual
diameter midpoint is within T=o(D) of one center. The explicit estimate
used in canonical audit.58 gives T<D/8 for D>=10000.

Suppose three actual loaded vertices v_i lie on a unit circle whose
center is strictly inside their triangle. Choose an actual diameter
partner a_i for each and set

    m_i=(v_i+a_i)/2, n_i=(v_i-a_i)/D.

All |n_i|=1 and

    D(n_i-n_j)=2(v_i-v_j)-2(m_i-m_j).

Since |v_i-v_j|<=2, every pair of these normals satisfies

    sin(theta_ij/2)<= (2+2T)/D < 1/4+2/10000 < 1/sqrt(2).

Thus their pairwise smaller angles are bounded by one alpha<pi/2.
Lift all three angles relative to the first into [-alpha,alpha]. The
extreme lifted difference is less than pi, so its pairwise bound forces
that difference to be at most alpha. The three normals lie in one
ordinary angular interval of width less than pi/2.

Each n_i uniquely supports the full source hull at v_i. In fact the
upper bound to its partner gives, for every other source point w,

    n_i dot(w-v_i)<=-|w-v_i|^2/(2D)<0.

Order the normals in the short interval. For the chord v_2-v_1, the
strict support inequalities make its oriented angle lie between the
quarter-turns of n_1 and n_2. The same applies to v_3-v_2 between the
quarter-turns of n_2 and n_3. Their forward chord directions differ by
less than pi/2. Therefore the triangle's interior angle at v_2 is
strictly greater than pi/2. But a circle center is strictly inside its
inscribed triangle only when that triangle is acute. Contradiction.

Hence, for D>=10000, no triple of actual loaded vertices can lie on a
unit circle with its center strictly inside their triangle.

Combined with Section 3, every feasible interior singleton replacement
in the generic isolate representative must have at least one retained
**unloaded core** unit neighbor. Its unit neighbors strictly surround it,
and every neighbor is in V by generic avoidance. If all were loaded,
triangulate their convex polygon. Some triangle contains the unit center,
possibly on an internal diagonal. Its circumcenter is therefore in the
closed triangle, so the triangle is nonobtuse. But the short-normal proof
above makes every triple of these loaded unit-circle points strictly
obtuse. This contradiction also handles the case of antipodal neighbors;
no unjustified choice of a strictly surrounding triple is needed.

In the primitive all-loaded shell V=B there is no such anchor. After
generic isolate normalization there is therefore no feasible interior
singleton replacement at all for a contact-active deletion, even with
arbitrary new contacts. This excludes a static exchange route; it does
not contradict existence of the source or eliminate its collective
packing/motion branch. The theorem does not forbid changed anchors,
multiple inserted points, sites outside the old hull, or a strict sub-D
competitor constructed differently.

## 7. Disposition

The static target is now a new surrounding unit-circle triple made of
actual core vertices, with all other source clearance and triangle-free
conditions certified, and enough removed contacts to pay its edges.
No audit.52--59 input cited here forces that triple. Existing three
neighbors at the deleted vertex and generic isolated near-unit blockers
cannot supply it. Collective packet and moving-anchor routes remain
available. No C-II branch, Level I quantifier or authoritative lifecycle
is closed by this normalization.

The proof uses finite polynomial avoidance and Euclidean circumcircle
identities. No numerical search or verifier is needed or claimed.
